No, don't share one `McpServer` across requests. In stateless mode (`sessionIdGenerator: undefined`), create a new `McpServer` and a new transport for every request, then close both when the request finishes.

**Why:**
- **Security:** A shared server instance can leak data between clients. The SDK maintainers' guidance is one `McpServer` per transport (advisory GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7).
- **The SDK now enforces it:** Newer SDK versions throw an error if you call `connect()` on a server that's already connected to a transport. Older examples that reuse one server and only create a new transport per request will hit this error. The search summary said this check arrived in 1.26.0, but the pages I opened didn't confirm that version number.
- **Cloudflare says the same:** "Create a fresh SDK v1 server for each request." Their `createMcpHandler` takes a server *factory* and "creates one MCP server for each request."

**Pattern (SDK v1):**
```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js";
import { z } from "zod";

// Constant inputs can live at module level
const echoSchema = { text: z.string() };

function buildServer() {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });
  server.tool("echo", echoSchema, async ({ text }) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text }],
  }));
  return server; // register everything before connect()
}

app.post("/mcp", async (req, res) => {
  const server = buildServer();
  const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
  res.on("close", () => {
    transport.close();
    server.close();
  });
  await server.connect(transport);
  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});
```

Only the `McpServer` and transport have to be new each time. Zod schemas, descriptions, DB pools and other config can stay shared. Building a server per request is cheap.

If you need state across requests, use stateful mode instead: set a `sessionIdGenerator`, keep a map from session ID to its server and transport, and give each session its own server.

My Ludion search found no lessons on this, so the answer is based on the web sources below.

Sources:
- [Cloudflare Agents – MCP handler API](https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/model-context-protocol/apis/handler-api/index.md)
- [MCP TypeScript SDK docs](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/)
- [mcp best practices (sharedcontext.ai)](https://sharedcontext.ai/skills/external/tenequm/mcp-best-practices)
- [Older shared-server example (mcpFirebird on Glama)](https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@PuroDelphi/mcpFirebird/blob/63cb1b6428e49e1db03f215e4db0a089618d1630/src/server/streamable-http.ts)

